# Question data for Book 5 Part 6 (linked lists). Entries: (stars, difficulty, prompt, asked-at). Every example is checked by check_examples.py.
LP="Linked-list pattern guides and question roundups (techinterview.org linked-list patterns page covering reverse, merge, cycle detection, fast-slow pointers and dummy nodes; a Substack linked-list prep post; techiedelight linked-list question list; a Questions100 linked-list category; DEV Community linked-list posts; search snippets only, not opened; WebFetch failed with getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND); tutorial content, not verified company reports"
CK="Candidate-reported rounds on Naukri Code360 (an Amazon on-campus March 2021 entry listing flatten of a multilevel list with child pointers; an Amazon December 2021 entry tied to adding two numbers held as lists; Flipkart 2020 and 2021 entries naming reverse linked list, one with an O(n) time O(1) space follow-up and one solved first with a stack; plus a LeetCode discuss Flipkart SDE-1 June 2024 report of a Reverse Linked List II variant given node values instead of positions; snippets only, not opened; candidate-reported, unverified, not evidence of current practice)"
FL="Floyd cycle-detection explanations (arXiv note on Pollard rho, Wikipedia cycle detection, VisuAlgo cycle finding, an Isabelle formalisation; snippets only, not opened: they give the mu + k = lambda*M relation, finding mu by restarting one pointer, finding lambda by looping once; none gives a complete written proof, so the proof here is the editor's own and is tested exhaustively)"
PY="Python recursion-limit explainers (note.nkmk.me, educative.io, DEV Community recursion-limit posts; snippets only, not opened: default limit 1000, raising it risks crashes, iteration preferred); __slots__ behaviour not found in any snippet and was verified by running Python 3.13 in the sandbox"
ED="Standard-problem lists known to the editor (not found as an opened page; included on the editor's knowledge of widely circulated problem sets; indicative only, no company attribution)"
def A(*k):
    d={'LP':LP,'CK':CK,'FL':FL,'PY':PY,'ED':ED}
    return ' | '.join(d[x] for x in k)
N="Nodes are `ListNode(val, next)`."
QS=[
# ===== 3 stars =====
(3,'🟢',f"Reverse a linked list. {N} Given the head of a singly linked list (0 <= len <= 5000), return the head of the reversed list. Write the iterative three-pointer version (`prev`, `cur`, and a saved `nxt`) in O(n) time and O(1) space, then the recursive version, and say why the recursive one uses O(n) stack. Reverse in place: the result must be made of the same node objects (no new nodes), which the tests check by identity. Example: [1,2,3,4,5] -> [5,4,3,2,1]; [1,2] -> [2,1]; [7] -> [7]; [] -> [].",A('LP','CK')),
(3,'🟢',f"Middle of a linked list. {N} Return the middle node of a singly linked list with 1 <= len <= 100, using one pass with a slow and a fast pointer. When the length is even there are two middles; return the SECOND one. Show that the loop condition `while fast and fast.next` yields the second middle, and say what changes to get the first middle. Return the node itself (the tests compare node identity, and print the values from that node onward). Example: [1,2,3,4,5] -> [3,4,5]; [1,2,3,4,5,6] -> [4,5,6]; [1] -> [1]; [1,2] -> [2].",A('LP','FL')),
(3,'🟢',f"Merge two sorted linked lists. {N} Given the heads of two lists, each sorted ascending (0 <= len <= 50 each), splice them into one sorted list and return its head, reusing the existing nodes (no new nodes; the tests check identity). Use a dummy head node and a tail pointer, and make the merge stable: on equal values the node from the FIRST list comes first. Explain what the dummy removes (the empty-result and first-node special cases) and why this is O(m + n) time and O(1) extra space. Example: [1,2,4] and [1,3,4] -> [1,1,2,3,4,4] (the first 1 is from the first list); [] and [] -> []; [] and [0] -> [0]; [2,5] and [1] -> [1,2,5].",A('LP','CK')),
(3,'🟢',f"Detect a cycle in a linked list. {N} Return True if following `next` from the head ever revisits a node, else False (0 <= len <= 10^4). Examples describe a cycle as an array plus `pos`, the index the tail's `next` points back to (-1 means no cycle). Give the O(n) time, O(n) space version with a set of node identities (`id(node)` or the node itself, since the default hash is identity), then Floyd's tortoise and hare in O(1) space. Explain why a fast pointer that moves 2 per step must catch a slow one inside a cycle and why it can never skip over it, and why a hash set of node VALUES would be wrong when values repeat. Example: [3,2,0,-4], pos=1 -> True; [1,2], pos=0 -> True; [1], pos=-1 -> False; [1,1,1], pos=-1 -> False; [], pos=-1 -> False.",A('LP','FL')),
(3,'🟡',f"Remove the n-th node from the end. {N} Given the head of a list with 1 <= len <= 30 and an integer n with 1 <= n <= len, delete the n-th node counted from the END and return the head, in ONE pass. Use a dummy node and two pointers kept n nodes apart, and explain why the dummy lets you delete the head node without a special case and why the gap is n (not n - 1 or n + 1) when you want the node BEFORE the one to delete. Also give the two-pass version (count, then walk) and compare. Example: [1,2,3,4,5], n=2 -> [1,2,3,5]; [1], n=1 -> []; [1,2], n=1 -> [1]; [1,2], n=2 -> [2].",A('LP','CK')),
(3,'🟡',f"Find where a cycle begins. {N} Given a list that may contain a cycle (examples give the array plus `pos`, the index the tail points back to, -1 for none), return the node where the cycle begins, or None. Use Floyd's two phases: find a meeting point with slow and fast, then restart one pointer at the head and move both one step at a time. PROVE why they meet exactly at the cycle start, with the tail length mu, the cycle length lambda and the distance k from the cycle start to the meeting point: state that the two pointers first meet after exactly t steps where t is the smallest POSITIVE multiple of lambda with t >= mu, derive mu + k = a multiple of lambda, and conclude. The same trick on an array of values is Part 1 Q28; here it is on node pointers. Example: [3,2,0,-4], pos=1 -> the node at index 1 (value 2); [1,2], pos=0 -> index 0; [1], pos=0 -> index 0 (a self-loop); [1,2,3], pos=-1 -> None.",A('FL','LP')),
(3,'🟡',f"Palindrome linked list. {N} Return True if the sequence of values reads the same forward and backward (1 <= len <= 10^5), using O(n) time and O(1) EXTRA space: find the middle with slow and fast pointers, reverse the second half in place, compare, and restore the list afterwards (so the input is unchanged, which the tests check by identity and by values). Explain why the O(n) space copy-to-array version is the baseline, how odd lengths are handled (skip the middle), and what goes wrong if you do not restore the list. Example: [1,2,2,1] -> True; [1,2,3,2,1] -> True; [1,2] -> False; [1] -> True; [1,0,0] -> False.",A('LP','ED')),
(3,'🟡',f"Add two numbers stored in reverse order. {N} Two non-empty lists (1 <= len <= 100, digits 0-9, no leading zeros except the number 0 itself) hold non-negative integers with the LEAST significant digit first. Return a new list for their sum in the same order. Walk both lists with a carry and a dummy head, and loop while either list or the carry is non-zero. Explain why a final carry can add a node and why you must not convert to an int (state what the int route costs and when it is fine in Python). Example: [2,4,3] and [5,6,4] -> [7,0,8] (342 + 465 = 807); [0] and [0] -> [0]; [9,9,9,9] and [1] -> [0,0,0,0,1]; [5] and [5] -> [0,1].",A('LP','CK')),
(3,'🟡',f"Reorder list. {N} Rearrange a list L0 -> L1 -> ... -> Ln-1 IN PLACE (1 <= len <= 5*10^4, node values may repeat; do not modify values, only links) into L0 -> Ln-1 -> L1 -> Ln-2 -> L2 -> ... Use three steps: find the middle, reverse the second half, interleave the two halves; state exactly where the list is cut so odd lengths put the extra node in the first half. O(n) time, O(1) space; the tests check node identity. Example: [1,2,3,4] -> [1,4,2,3]; [1,2,3,4,5] -> [1,5,2,4,3]; [1] -> [1]; [1,2] -> [1,2].",A('LP','ED')),
(3,'🟡',f"Intersection of two linked lists. {N} Two lists may share a common tail (the same node objects from some point on, not merely equal values). Return the first shared node, or None, in O(m + n) time and O(1) space without modifying either list. Examples give `prefixA`, `prefixB` and `shared` arrays: A is prefixA followed by the shared nodes, B is prefixB followed by the SAME shared nodes. Give the length-difference method and the two-pointer switch trick (when a pointer reaches the end, restart it on the other list's head) and explain why both pointers travel m + n steps and meet at the intersection or both reach None together. Say why comparing `val` is wrong. Example: prefixA [4,1], prefixB [5,6,1], shared [8,4,5] -> the node with value 8; prefixA [1,9,1], prefixB [3], shared [2,4] -> value 2; prefixA [2,6,4], prefixB [1,5], shared [] -> None; prefixA [], prefixB [], shared [7] -> value 7.",A('LP','ED')),
(3,'🟡',f"Copy a list with random pointers. A list node is `Node(val, next, random)` where `random` points to any node in the list or None (0 <= len <= 1000). Return a DEEP copy: new nodes with the same values whose `next` and `random` point only to new nodes (the tests check that no node of the copy is a node of the original, and that the pointer structure matches). Examples write each node as [val, random_index], where random_index is the position of its random target or None. Give the hash-map solution (original node to copy, two passes, O(n) space) and explain why a map keyed by node identity is needed rather than by value. The O(1)-extra-space variant is a separate question later in this part. Example: [[7,None],[13,0],[11,4],[10,2],[1,0]] -> an equal-structure copy; [[1,1],[2,1]] -> an equal-structure copy; [] -> [].",A('LP','ED')),
(3,'🔴',f"Merge k sorted linked lists. {N} `lists` holds k sorted lists (0 <= k <= 100, total nodes N <= 10^4, a list may be empty). Return one sorted list reusing the nodes; ties keep the lower list index first (stable). Give three solutions and their costs: merging one list into an accumulated result (O(kN)), a `heapq` of (value, list index, node) used only as the merge tool (O(N log k); heap theory itself is Part 8; say why the list index must be in the tuple, since two ListNode objects are not orderable), and pairwise divide and conquer (O(N log k)). Explain how the heap holds at most k nodes. Example: [[1,4,5],[1,3,4],[2,6]] -> [1,1,2,3,4,4,5,6]; [] -> []; [[]] -> []; [[],[2],[]] -> [2].",A('LP','CK')),
(3,'🔴',f"Reverse nodes in k-groups. {N} Given a list (1 <= len <= 5000) and an integer k with 1 <= k <= len, reverse the nodes k at a time and return the new head; if fewer than k nodes remain at the end, leave that last group as it is. Only links may change, not values (tests check identity). Use a dummy head, a `group_prev` pointer, a check that k nodes remain before reversing, and a reversal of exactly k nodes that reconnects both ends. O(n) time, O(1) space; mention the recursive version and its O(n/k) stack. Example: [1,2,3,4,5], k=2 -> [2,1,4,3,5]; [1,2,3,4,5], k=3 -> [3,2,1,4,5]; [1,2,3], k=1 -> [1,2,3]; [1,2,3,4], k=4 -> [4,3,2,1].",A('LP','CK')),
# ===== 2 stars =====
(2,'🟢',f"Delete a node when you are given only that node. {N} You get a reference `node` that is in a singly linked list but is NOT the tail (the list has at least 2 nodes), and no head. Delete it from the list in O(1). The trick is to copy the next node's value into this node and bypass the next node; explain why this is not a true deletion of the given node object, what it breaks (other references to the next node, identity checks) and why it cannot work for the tail. Example: [4,5,1,9] deleting the node with value 5 -> [4,1,9]; [4,5,1,9] deleting the node with value 1 -> [4,5,9]; [1,2] deleting the node with value 1 -> [2]; [0,1,2,3] deleting the node with value 0 -> [1,2,3].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟢',f"Remove duplicates from a sorted linked list. {N} The list is sorted ascending (0 <= len <= 300). Delete nodes so each value appears once (keep the FIRST node of each run) and return the head, in place. Explain why you only need to compare a node with its successor, why you do not advance when you delete, and why no dummy is needed here (the head never changes) while it is needed for the 'remove all duplicated values' variant that is a separate question. Example: [1,1,2] -> [1,2]; [1,1,2,3,3] -> [1,2,3]; [5,5,5] -> [5]; [] -> []; [1,2,3] -> [1,2,3].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟢',f"The dummy (sentinel) node technique: remove all nodes with a given value. {N} Given a list (0 <= len <= 10^4) and an integer `val`, delete every node whose value equals `val` and return the head, in place. Write it with a dummy node in front, and then without one (a head-skipping loop followed by the main loop) and compare how much special-case code each needs. State what a dummy node costs (one extra node, O(1)) and name two other list problems where it removes a head case. Example: [1,2,6,3,4,5,6], val=6 -> [1,2,3,4,5]; [], val=1 -> []; [7,7,7], val=7 -> []; [1,2], val=3 -> [1,2]; [6,1], val=6 -> [1].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟢',f"Python specifics: the node class, identity versus equality, building and printing lists. Write `ListNode` with `__slots__ = ('val', 'next')` and helper functions `from_array(arr)` (returns the head, building from the back or with a dummy), `to_array(head)` and a cycle-safe `show(head)`. Explain what `__slots__` removes (the per-instance `__dict__`, so no new attributes can be added) and why it saves memory; show that `a == b` is False for two nodes with equal values (default equality is identity) and say what `is` tests; explain why a `show` that loops `while node:` hangs on a cyclic list and how a set of visited ids or a step cap fixes it. `show` must print values joined by ' -> ' and end with ' -> None' for a normal list, 'None' for an empty list, and for a cycle stop at the repeated node and end with ' -> (cycle to index k)' where k is the index of the repeated node. Example: show of [1,2,3] -> '1 -> 2 -> 3 -> None'; show of [] -> 'None'; show of [1,2,3] with the tail pointing back to index 1 -> '1 -> 2 -> 3 -> (cycle to index 1)'; to_array(from_array([4,5])) -> [4,5].",A('PY','LP')),
(2,'🟡',f"Reverse a sublist between positions. {N} Given a list (1 <= len <= 500) and 1-based positions left <= right <= len, reverse only the nodes from position left to position right, in place, in one pass, and return the head. Use a dummy node, walk to the node before position left, then repeatedly move the node after the current tail of the reversed part to the front of that part ('head insertion'); explain why it is one pass and O(1) space, and why left == right is a no-op. Also say how the node-VALUES variant of the same problem (a candidate-reported Flipkart variant) changes the first step. Example: [1,2,3,4,5], left=2, right=4 -> [1,4,3,2,5]; [5], 1, 1 -> [5]; [3,5], 1, 2 -> [5,3]; [1,2,3], 1, 3 -> [3,2,1]; [1,2,3], 2, 2 -> [1,2,3].",A('CK','LP')),
(2,'🟡',f"Swap nodes in pairs. {N} Given a list (0 <= len <= 100), swap every two adjacent nodes by changing LINKS, not values (tests check identity: the node objects themselves must move), and return the new head. A last unpaired node stays. Write it iteratively with a dummy and three pointers per pair, and recursively, and say what each rewires and why the order of the three assignments matters. Example: [1,2,3,4] -> [2,1,4,3]; [1,2,3] -> [2,1,3]; [1] -> [1]; [] -> []; [1,2] -> [2,1].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Odd-even list. {N} Group all nodes at ODD positions (1-based) first, then those at EVEN positions, keeping the relative order inside each group; this is about positions, not values. Do it in place in O(n) time and O(1) extra space (nodes are reused, so the tests check identity) with two chains and a joining step. Explain the loop condition `even and even.next` and what is wrong with testing odd instead, and where the odd tail connects to the even head. Example: [1,2,3,4,5] -> [1,3,5,2,4]; [2,1,3,5,6,4,7] -> [2,3,6,7,1,5,4]; [1] -> [1]; [1,2] -> [1,2]; [] -> [].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Partition a list around a value. {N} Given a list (0 <= len <= 200) and an integer x, rearrange so that all nodes with value < x come before all nodes with value >= x, preserving the original relative order inside each part (a stable partition), reusing the nodes. Build two chains with two dummy heads, then join them and terminate the second chain with None; explain why forgetting that last step can create a cycle. O(n) time, O(1) extra space. Example: [1,4,3,2,5,2], x=3 -> [1,2,2,4,3,5]; [2,1], x=2 -> [1,2]; [], x=0 -> []; [3,3], x=3 -> [3,3]; [1,2,3], x=4 -> [1,2,3].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Rotate a list to the right. {N} Rotate a list (0 <= len <= 500) to the right by k places, 0 <= k <= 2*10^9, and return the head: the last k nodes (k taken modulo the length) move to the front. Find the length and the tail in one pass, reduce k % n, then cut at position n - k % n; you may close the list into a ring temporarily. O(n) time, O(1) space; explain why k can be huge but the work cannot depend on k, and handle empty lists, single nodes and k % n == 0. Example: [1,2,3,4,5], k=2 -> [4,5,1,2,3]; [0,1,2], k=4 -> [2,0,1]; [1], k=99 -> [1]; [1,2], k=2 -> [1,2]; [] , k=3 -> []; [1,2,3], k=2000000000 -> [2,3,1].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Add two numbers stored in forward order. {N} Two non-empty lists (1 <= len <= 100, digits 0-9, no leading zeros except 0 itself) hold non-negative integers with the MOST significant digit first. Return their sum as a new list in the same order WITHOUT reversing the inputs. Give two ways: push the digits on two stacks (Python lists) and add from the end building the result by head insertion, and reverse-add-reverse (which mutates the inputs unless you restore them); say which preserves the inputs and what each costs in space. Example: [7,2,4,3] and [5,6,4] -> [7,8,0,7] (7243 + 564 = 7807); [2,4,3] and [5,6,4] -> [8,0,7]; [0] and [0] -> [0]; [9,9] and [1] -> [1,0,0].",A('LP','CK')),
(2,'🟡',f"Remove ALL values that are duplicated in a sorted list. {N} The list is sorted ascending (0 <= len <= 300). Delete every node whose value appears more than once so that only values that occur exactly once remain; return the head, in place. Use a dummy head, a `prev` pointer that trails the last kept node, and skip each run of equal values entirely; explain why the dummy is required here and how this differs from keeping one copy of each value. Example: [1,2,3,3,4,4,5] -> [1,2,5]; [1,1,1,2,3] -> [2,3]; [1,1] -> []; [1,2,3] -> [1,2,3]; [] -> [].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Sort a list with merge sort in O(1) extra space. {N} Sort a list (0 <= len <= 5*10^4) ascending, stably (equal values keep their original order, tests check by identity), in O(n log n) time. Give the top-down merge sort (find the middle with slow and fast, cut, sort both halves, merge) and state its stack depth; then the BOTTOM-UP version with O(1) extra space: merge runs of size 1, 2, 4, ... using a `split(head, size)` helper that cuts off `size` nodes and a merge that returns head and tail. Say why arrays-style quicksort and index-based heap sort do not fit a list and why sorting a Python list copy is a valid but different answer (O(n) space). Example: [4,2,1,3] -> [1,2,3,4]; [-1,5,3,4,0] -> [-1,0,3,4,5]; [] -> []; [2,2,1,1] -> [1,1,2,2]; [1] -> [1].",A('LP','ED')),
(2,'🟡',f"Why a recursive list algorithm fails on long lists in Python. The recursive reverse `def rev(h): ... rev(h.next) ...` works on a list of 100 nodes but raises `RecursionError` on a list of 2000 nodes under the default recursion limit, while the iterative version handles 10^5 nodes. Write both, demonstrate the failure, and explain: Python's default limit (1000 frames; read it with `sys.getrecursionlimit()`), that the depth equals the list length here, why raising it with `sys.setrecursionlimit` is a risk to weigh (stack use, crashes) rather than a fix to rely on, and why the same applies to recursive printing, recursive length and recursive merge. State which common list algorithms are naturally recursive-friendly only for short inputs. Example: recursive reverse of [1,2,3,4,5] -> [5,4,3,2,1]; recursive reverse of a 100-node list works; recursive reverse of a 2000-node list under the default limit -> RecursionError; iterative reverse of a 2000-node list -> works.",A('PY','LP')),
# ===== 1 star =====
(1,'🟡',f"Remove duplicates from an UNSORTED list. {N} Given a list of integers (0 <= len <= 2000, unsorted, values may repeat), keep only the FIRST occurrence of each value and return the head, in place. Give the O(n) time, O(n) space version with a `set` of seen values, and the O(1)-space O(n^2) version that for each node scans the rest of the list to unlink later copies; explain the trade-off and why sorting first changes the surviving order. Example: [1,2,1,3,2,4] -> [1,2,3,4]; [5,5,5] -> [5]; [] -> []; [3,1,2] -> [3,1,2]; [2,1,2,1] -> [2,1].",A('LP','ED')),
(1,'🟡',f"Insertion sort on a linked list. {N} Sort a list (0 <= len <= 1500) ascending by insertion sort: take nodes one at a time from the input and insert each into the sorted part built behind a dummy head, reusing the nodes. The sort must be stable (equal values keep original order, tests check by identity). State the cost (O(n^2) worst case, O(n) when already sorted if you add the 'compare with the last sorted node first' shortcut), and why insertion is cheap on a list (no shifting) but finding the place still takes a walk. Example: [4,2,1,3] -> [1,2,3,4]; [-1,5,3,4,0] -> [-1,0,3,4,5]; [] -> []; [2,2,1] -> [1,2,2]; [1,2,3] -> [1,2,3].",A('LP','ED')),
(1,'🟡',f"Doubly linked list operations with sentinels. Implement a doubly linked list with `prev` and `next` pointers and two sentinel nodes `head` and `tail` (the sentinels hold no data and are never removed) supporting `add_first(v)`, `add_last(v)`, `remove(node)` in O(1) given the node, `pop_first()`, `pop_last()` and `to_array()`. Explain why sentinels remove every empty-list and one-node special case, and the four pointer assignments needed for an insert between two nodes and for a removal. The tests check that following `next` forward and `prev` backward give mirror-image sequences after every operation. Example: add_last 1, add_last 2, add_first 0 -> [0,1,2]; then pop_last -> returns 2, list [0,1]; then pop_first -> returns 0, list [1]; then pop_first, then to_array -> [] (and a further pop returns None).",A('LP','ED')),
(1,'🟡',f"LRU cache: the linked-list idea (concept only). A cache of capacity c supports `get(key)` and `put(key, value)` in O(1), evicting the least recently used key when full; a `get` or `put` of a key makes it the most recently used. Explain at concept level why a hash map alone cannot find the least recently used key in O(1) and a list alone cannot find a key in O(1), how a doubly linked list (most recent at the head side, with sentinels) plus a dict key -> node gives O(1) for both, what pointer moves `get` and `put` perform, and why a SINGLY linked list is not enough (you need the previous node to unlink). Mention that `collections.OrderedDict` does the same internally and that the full class design and thread-safety discussion belong to the design part of this book (Part 14). Example with capacity 2: put(1,1), put(2,2), get(1) -> 1, put(3,3) evicts key 2, get(2) -> -1, put(4,4) evicts key 1, get(1) -> -1, get(3) -> 3, get(4) -> 4.",A('LP','ED')),
(1,'🟡',f"Flatten a multilevel doubly linked list. Each node has `val`, `prev`, `next` and `child` (a child is the head of a separate doubly linked list, which may have children of its own); total nodes <= 1000. Flatten into a single-level doubly linked list in place, where a node's child list is spliced in right after the node (depth-first order) and every `child` becomes None and every `prev` pointer is correct. Examples write the structure as nested arrays: a list item that is itself an array is the child list of the node just before it. Give the iterative stack-free splice (find the child's tail, connect it to the next node) and a recursive version, and mention its depth risk. Example: [1,2,3,[7,8,[11,12],9],4] -> [1,2,3,7,8,11,12,9,4]; [1,[2],3] -> [1,2,3]; [] -> []; [1,[2,[3]]] -> [1,2,3]; [1,2] -> [1,2].",A('CK','LP')),
(1,'🔴',f"Cycle length and tail length with Floyd. {N} For a list with possible cycle (examples give the array plus `pos`, the index the tail points back to, -1 for none), return the pair (mu, lambda): mu is the number of nodes before the cycle starts and lambda is the number of nodes in the cycle; if there is no cycle return (n, 0) where n is the list length. Find the meeting point, then count the cycle by walking one pointer around once, then find mu by the restart-from-head step; give the total number of pointer moves as a bound in terms of mu and lambda and derive it (do not use loose phrases). Contrast with Brent's variant at one sentence. The cycle-in-an-array-of-jumps version is Part 2 Q44. Example: [3,2,0,-4], pos=1 -> (1, 3); [1,2], pos=0 -> (0, 2); [1], pos=-1 -> (1, 0); [1,2,3], pos=2 -> (2, 1); [], pos=-1 -> (0, 0).",A('FL','LP')),
(1,'🔴',f"Break a cycle (remove the loop). {N} Given a list that may contain a cycle (array plus `pos` for the example, -1 for none), remove the cycle IN PLACE by setting `next = None` on the last node of the cycle, using O(1) extra space, and return the index of the node whose `next` you changed (None if there was no cycle). Use Floyd to find the cycle start, then walk the cycle until the node whose next is the start; explain the self-loop case where the start itself is that node. The tests check that the result is acyclic, has the same nodes in the same order and no node was created. Example: [3,2,0,-4], pos=1 -> cut at index 3, list [3,2,0,-4]; [1,2], pos=0 -> cut at index 1, list [1,2]; [1,2,3], pos=2 -> cut at index 2 (self-loop), list [1,2,3]; [1], pos=-1 -> None, list [1].",A('FL','LP')),
(1,'🔴',f"Copy a list with random pointers in O(1) extra space. A node is `Node(val, next, random)`, `random` points to any node or None (0 <= len <= 1000). Return a deep copy WITHOUT a hash map: (1) interleave, inserting each copy right after its original, (2) set each copy's `random` to `original.random.next`, (3) unweave the two lists and, so that the INPUT list is restored exactly, put the original nodes back in their original order. State why step 2 is correct only after step 1 and why the order of the steps matters; state the extra space as O(1) apart from the output nodes. Examples write each node as [val, random_index]. Example: [[7,None],[13,0],[11,4],[10,2],[1,0]] -> an equal-structure copy and the input unchanged; [[1,1],[2,1]] -> an equal-structure copy; [[5,0]] -> a copy whose random points to itself; [] -> [].",A('LP','ED')),
(1,'🟡',f"Testing linked-list solutions: a harness. Describe and write the test harness used for this part: `from_array`, `to_array`, `same_structure(a, b)` comparing two lists by values, a cycle check (Floyd, O(1) space) so a broken solution fails instead of hanging, an aliasing check that two lists share no node (a set of `id`), and an in-place check that records `id` of every input node before the call and verifies afterwards that the result is made of exactly those node objects (for questions that say 'in place'). Explain why testing by values alone cannot catch an algorithm that builds new nodes, why a step cap is needed when a bug creates a cycle, and how to compare nodes in a random cross-check against a brute-force on Python lists. Example: from_array([1,2,3]) -> 3 distinct nodes and to_array gives [1,2,3]; reversing it in place -> [3,2,1] with the same 3 ids, so the in-place check is True; a reverse that builds new nodes -> [3,2,1] by values but the in-place check is False; a bug that links the tail to the head -> reported as a cycle instead of hanging.",A('PY','LP')),
]
